            "currentUpdate": "CT showed gas and fluid beside the proximal sleeve with fluid extending into the peritoneal cavity. During the hospital assessment her pressure fell to 84/52 mmHg, pulse rose to 132/min and diffuse guarding developed. Resuscitation and antimicrobial treatment were underway.",
            "stem": "Which source-control approach best fits {patientName}'s condition at this point in the hospital record?",
            "answerChoices": [
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.bariatric-metabolic.deterioration-source-control.percutaneous",
                "label": "Percutaneous drainage of the perigastric collection",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "Drainage can contribute to care of a stable contained collection, but a localized catheter plan does not match the recorded shock and diffuse peritoneal findings.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.bariatric-metabolic.deterioration-source-control.explore",
                "label": "Urgent operative exploration with washout and drainage",
                "isCorrect": true,
                "rationale": "Deteriorating circulation and diffuse guarding require urgent operative source control while resuscitation continues.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.bariatric-metabolic.deterioration-source-control.endoscopic",
                "label": "Endoscopic defect treatment with internal drainage",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "An expert endoscopic approach is a real option for selected stable leaks. It should not delay surgery for the unstable peritonitic course shown here.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              }
            ],
            "shuffleAnswers": true,
            "explanation": "The new circulation and examination findings change the source-control route. Urgent operative exploration is required in the recorded unstable peritonitic phase; resuscitation and antimicrobials continue in parallel. A stable-leak endoscopic or localized drainage strategy should not delay that response.",
